## 1. Result and Boundary

Let

\[
0\longrightarrow E_0\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X^{\oplus2}
\longrightarrow i_*W\longrightarrow0,
\qquad
W=\omega_\Gamma\otimes\mathcal O_X(-H)|_\Gamma,
\]

be the dual Hartshorne-Serre sequence for
\(\Gamma=\Gamma_0=(r=aT_0=0)\), and put
\(L_0=\mathcal O_X(-H)\). For the clock stabilizer
\(Z\simeq\mathbb Z/3\), with \(\chi(Z)=\omega\), the reverse extension
space is exactly

\[
R_0:=\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_0,E_0)
\simeq 3\bigl(\mathbf 1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2\bigr). \tag{1}
\]

Consequently

\[
\dim R_0=9,
\qquad
\dim R_0^Z=3. \tag{2}
\]

The reverse branch is nevertheless excluded from the current physical
endpoint contract. Every extension

\[
0\longrightarrow E_0\longrightarrow K_\xi\longrightarrow L_0
\longrightarrow0 \tag{3}
\]

is locally split, is not locally free along \(\Gamma\), and has torsion in
its ordinary restriction to a generic fiber meeting a horizontal component
of \(\Gamma\). It therefore cannot equal the locally free, stable G3G
special-fiber repair.

This closes the reverse-extension test requested after UST.G3I. It does not
construct the physical hidden \(W_9\). Within the Hartshorne-Serre repair
program, the remaining route is the direct UST.G3H construction: one globally perfect repaired
\(\alpha\)-twisted object with two global qutrit generator chain maps.

## 2. Clock Representation of the Canonical Space

The normalization of \(\Gamma\) is the disjoint union of five elliptic
curves: two vertical fibers \(V_+,V_-\) and three horizontal sections
\(H_0,H_1,H_2\). Its dual graph is \(K_{2,3}\).

The clock fixes each vertical component and acts there by translation, so
their holomorphic one-form directions are trivial representations. It
cyclically permutes the three horizontal components, giving the regular
representation

\[
\operatorname{Reg}_{\mathbb Z/3}=\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2.
\]

The two-dimensional cycle space of \(K_{2,3}\) is the augmentation
representation \(\chi\oplus\chi^2\). The normalization sequence, followed
by Serre duality on the connected Gorenstein curve, therefore gives

\[
H^0(\Gamma,\omega_\Gamma)
\simeq3\mathbf1\oplus2\chi\oplus2\chi^2. \tag{4}
\]

The multiplicities sum to seven, as required by the arithmetic genus.

## 3. Exact Rank of the Six-to-Seven Map

Twisting the dual sequence by \(\mathcal O_X(H)\) gives

\[
0\longrightarrow E_0(H)\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X(H)^{\oplus2}
\xrightarrow{\mu}i_*\omega_\Gamma\longrightarrow0. \tag{5}
\]

The two Hartshorne-Serre generators are \(T_1,T_2\), with the two
nontrivial clock characters. Since \(H\) is pulled back from the base,
\(H^0(X,\mathcal O_X(H))\) is clock-trivial and has dimension three. Thus

\[
H^0(X,\mathcal O_X(H))^{\oplus2}
\simeq3\chi\oplus3\chi^2. \tag{6}
\]

Let \(r\) be the section cutting out the elliptic plane section \(E_B\).
For either \(T_i\), multiplication on \(\Gamma\) has kernel exactly
\(\mathbb C r\). Indeed, \(r\) vanishes on every component of \(\Gamma\),
while \(T_1\) and \(T_2\) are nonzero on all three horizontal qutrit
sections. A base hyperplane section whose product vanishes must therefore
vanish on \(E_B\), hence is a multiple of \(r\). Each character block of
\(\mu\) has rank two and exhausts the corresponding target block in (4).
It follows that

\[
\ker\mu\simeq\chi\oplus\chi^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{coker}\mu\simeq3\mathbf1. \tag{7}
\]

This upgrades the G3I lower bound from one to the exact cokernel dimension
three.

## 4. Exact Reverse Ext Dimension

For the principal elliptic fibration, the locked Leray data are

\[
R^0\pi_*\mathcal O_X=R^1\pi_*\mathcal O_X=\mathcal O_S.
\]

Kodaira vanishing on the degree-two K3 base and
\(H^2(S,\mathcal O_S(H))=0\) give

\[
H^1(X,\mathcal O_X(H))\simeq H^0(S,\mathcal O_S(H)),
\qquad h^1(X,\mathcal O_X(H))=3. \tag{8}
\]

Fiber translation acts trivially on this group. After the two generator
linearizations, the next source in the long exact sequence is therefore
\(3\chi\oplus3\chi^2\). On the other hand,

\[
H^1(\Gamma,\omega_\Gamma)
\simeq H^0(\Gamma,\mathcal O_\Gamma)^*\simeq\mathbf1. \tag{9}
\]

The equivariant map from the nontrivial characters to (9) is zero. The long
exact sequence of (5) consequently contains

\[
0\longrightarrow3\mathbf1\longrightarrow R_0
\longrightarrow3\chi\oplus3\chi^2\longrightarrow0. \tag{10}
\]

Representations of the finite clock group over \(\mathbb C\) are semisimple,
so (10) splits as a representation. This proves (1) and (2). In particular,
clock invariance alone leaves a projective plane of nonzero classes; it does
not select one class.

## 5. Shift, Shared-Line and Analytic-Alpha Typing

The second qutrit generator does not stabilize \(\Gamma_0\). It transports

\[
(\Gamma_0,E_0,R_0)\longrightarrow(\Gamma_1,E_1,R_1)
\longrightarrow(\Gamma_2,E_2,R_2)\longrightarrow(\Gamma_0,E_0,R_0). \tag{11}
\]

Thus the literal shift, and hence the full two-direction \(E[3]\) or
shared-line requirement, is not an endomorphism of the single space \(R_0\).
A full projective action is defined only after choosing the global orbit
chain identifications. Those are precisely the two UST.G3H generator maps
that remain open. Treating the transport in (11) as a pre-existing action on
\(R_0\) would assume the theorem being sought.

The analytic BHT class \(\alpha\) is likewise not a character acting on the
source Ext space. It is the Cech twisting law of objects and chain maps in
\(D^b(J,\alpha)\). Under the BHT equivalence it types the transformed Ext
group, but its effect is carried by overlap maps, not by an additional scalar
eigenvalue on \(R_0\). The finite multiplier \(\sigma\) and analytic
\(\alpha\) therefore remain distinct, as required by UST.G3G and UST.G3H.

## 6. Local-Freeness and Fiber No-Go

Fix a point of \(\Gamma\), let \(R=\mathcal O_{X,p}\), and let
\(I=(f,g)\) be the codimension-two lci ideal of \(\Gamma\). Because the two
Hartshorne-Serre sections generate the line \(W\), a local change of basis
puts the surjection \(R^2\to R/I\) in the form \((1,0)\). Hence

\[
(E_0)_p\simeq I\oplus R. \tag{12}
\]

The ideal of a nonempty codimension-two regular embedding needs two minimal
generators and is not a free rank-one \(R\)-module. Therefore \(E_0\) is not
locally free along \(\Gamma\).

Since \((L_0)_p\simeq R\) is projective, every stalk of (3) splits:

\[
(K_\xi)_p\simeq(E_0)_p\oplus R\simeq I\oplus R^2. \tag{13}
\]

No value of \(\xi\) changes this local module. Every reverse middle sheaf is
perfect of projective dimension one, but none is locally free.

The failure is also visible on a generic fiber meeting a horizontal
component. In local coordinates choose \(f\) as the base equation and \(g\)
as the transverse fiber coordinate. Tensoring the Koszul presentation

\[
0\longrightarrow R\xrightarrow{(-g,f)}R^2\longrightarrow I\longrightarrow0
\]

with the fiber ring \(A\) sets \(f=0\) and gives

\[
I\otimes_R A\simeq A\oplus A/(g),
\qquad
K_\xi\otimes_R A\simeq A^3\oplus A/(g). \tag{14}
\]

The torsion summand is independent of \(\xi\). By contrast, the G3G repair is
a locally free rank-three fiber object whose rank-two factor is the
non-split stable bundle \(Q^\vee\otimes N\). Therefore no reverse extension
passes the required G3G fiber test. Any subsequent surgery that removes
(14) is a direct repaired-complex construction, not selection of a reverse
extension class.

